345 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 231 0 R
/E (During the brainstorming stage, the team needed to focus on specific criteria to develop a feasible plan. Since the fund would depend on grants, donations, or partnerships, the model needed to include spending limits, approval processes, and realistic funding pathways. Potential solutions were evaluated based on feasibility, inclusivity, and originality to ensure standards were maintained in the development of the YCCF. )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
346 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 231 0 R
/E (The team considered multiple alternatives before selecting the YCCF. One early idea was a standardized exit strategy for youth transitioning out of homelessness services, which would require coordination across multiple service providers, funders, and government systems. A second idea was a housing + employment pathway, although it would require substantial subsidies, landlord partnerships, employment placements, and administration to realize. These alternative ideas helped clarify that the strongest solution would be a flexible fund supported by practical tools, which was a tangible goal as opposed to broad systemic reforms. )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
347 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 231 0 R
/E (While the true scope of the YCCF was not implemented during the course, the team ensured to use informed decision-making to validate the feasibility of the project. Conducted research helped to confirm the importance of social inclusion for youth, and partner feedback and site visits helped to shape the project by identifying community participation and discretionary funding as practical areas of need. The final direction that the YCCF took aimed to strike a balance between ambition and practicality, attempting to holistically support community belonging and support rather than solving youth homelessness as a whole. )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
