<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 233 0 R
/E (Ethics considerations began with filling out an ethics roadmap, as shown below. Through this, it was shown that our work did not involve human participant research; those of vulnerable populations were not directly involved in this project. Rather, it analyzed other, already public, research and data.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
368 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 233 0 R
/T (Ethics Decision Process)
/E (Ethics Decision Process)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
369 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 233 0 R
/T (Ethics of Care)
/E (Ethics of Care)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
370 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 233 0 R
/E (Throughout the project, we focused on maintaining an ethics of care, but for the project itself as related to its goals, as well as within ourselves as we worked on the project. We emphasized empathy, interpersonal connections, and context over rigidity and universal rules. For example, one of our original considerations for a solution involved the standardization of rules. During one feedback session, we were made aware that it may be a solution that involves ignoring context. With that awareness in mind, our project changed course to a more context-aware solution. Each time we got more feedback, we tweaked things to better align with core values such as inclusivity, diversity, and equity. This process is highlighted in several different sections of the implementation plan, such as in the risks and mitigations section.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
